<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Batten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Batten is a small peninsula on the eastern side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Excavations have shown it was a trading port in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, linking south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with Brittany and the Atlantic coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the exchange of metals and other goods. In the 20th century it was the site of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> flying-boat station.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-batten.jpg" alt="Mount Batten" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trysca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountbattenpeninsula.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Batten is a rocky outcrop 24 metres tall on a peninsula about 600 metres long in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a>. It was once called How Stert and takes its present name from Sir William Batten, MP and Surveyor of the Navy. To the west the Mount Batten Breakwater, built by the Cattewater Commissioners between 1878 and 1881 at a cost of £20,000 and 915 feet long, shelters the Cattewater and Sutton Harbour. Before it and the Plymouth Breakwater of 1841 were built the peninsula suffered heavy erosion, and its isthmus was breached in 1633 and 1634.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations reported by Barry Cunliffe in 1988 showed that the site traded with the continent from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, peaked in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and remained in use through the Roman era. It seems to have been a main route by which goods such as wine entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and it has been suggested as the trading centre Ictis named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>. Three British-made bronze mirrors found there were lost in the Plymouth Blitz.</p><p>In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the peninsula became a defensive point for Plymouth, commanding the Cattewater, and in 1652 the circular artillery fort of Mount Batten Tower was built. Quarrying from 1839 to the mid-1860s supplied stone for the steam yards at Devonport and threatened the tower until the landowner, Lord Morley, restricted it after a petition from 53 citizens. Seaplane trials began in 1913, and the air station was RNAS Cattewater, then RAF Cattewater and from 1928 RAF Mount Batten. T. E. Lawrence served there under the name Aircraftsman Shaw, and Royal Australian Air Force Sunderland flying boats flew from it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>. The RAF left in 1986.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the Plymouth Development Corporation took over the site, the peninsula gained a marina and a centre for sea sports, and the refurbished breakwater reopened to the public in 1995. Mount Batten Tower is a Scheduled Ancient Monument. Nearby lies the Cattewater Wreck, a merchant ship of the early 16th century and the first wreck protected under the Protection of Wrecks Act 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Batten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek writers from Pytheas onwards describe the tin trade of the far south-west, and harbours such as <b>Mount Batten</b> on Plymouth Sound linked the region with Brittany and the wider Atlantic world.</blockquote><a href="/state/dumnonii">Dumnonii</a></li></ul></section>
